.TH yamn 1 "21 Oct 2026" "version 0.2.7+gw1"

.SH NAME
yamn - An anonymous remailer application

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yamn -m [-S] [-l rem1,rem2,rem3,...] [-t user@host] [-s subject] [-c num] [[recipient] filename]
.PP
.B yamn -M [-D] [-S]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Yamn (Yet another Mix network) is  an  anonymous  remailer.  Remailers  provide
protection against   traffic  analysis  and  allow  sending  mail  anonymously
or pseudonymously.

In the non-interactive mode, Yamn reads a message from  its  standard  input
or from a Maildir.  Destination address and input file can be specified in the
command line.  If no address is given in  the  command line,  the  input  file
is expected to contain a message complete with mail headers.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "-c, --copies=\fInum"
When operating in client mode, define how many copies of each message should be
sent (1 to 5).  Multiple copies share the same exit-remailer which retains a list of
processed Packet-IDs, ensuring that only a single copy is delivered.  This setting overrides the
config option
.B "stats.num_copies"
(default: 2).
.TP
.B "--config=\fIfilename"
Use this configuration file.  It must exist: yamn doesn't fall back to
another one.  See
.B CONFIGURATION
for where yamn looks without this option.
.TP
.B "-d, --dummy"
Send a dummy message through a random chain of two remailers.  The dummies
sent along with a message
.RB ( -m )
take such a chain too, not the message's
.BR --chain .
.TP
.B "--debug"
Print the configuration in use, with all defaults filled in, and exit.
The SMTP password is shown as (hidden).
.TP
.B "--dir=\fIdirectory"
Base directory for yamn's files: the relative paths of the
.B files
config options, the defaults included, are relative to it, and
.I directory/yamn.yml
is one of the places the configuration is looked for.  See
.B "Where the files are"
below for the paths without this option.
.TP
.B "-D, --daemon"
Start a remailer in an endless loop of reading, processing and sending
messages.  This option only has meaning when used with the
.B "-M"
option.
.TP
.B "--keep-headers"
In client mode, send the message's headers as they are.  Without it, yamn
removes headers that could identify you before encrypting the message: anything
an exit wouldn't pass on anyway, plus Date, Message-ID, From and Reply-To,
and headers with a CR or NUL in them (an exit refuses those).  It
reports what it removed.  The exit writes its own From, so the message is
still delivered with one.
.TP
.B "--keep-from"
In client mode, send the message's From header.  Most exits keep only the
name from it and put their own address after it, so a From such as
.I "Bob <x@example.invalid>"
signs the message with a pseudonym.  An exit with
.B custom_from
enabled delivers the address too.
.TP
.B "--keep-reply-to"
In client mode, send the message's Reply-To header.  Every exit delivers
it unchanged, so use it only with an address that doesn't identify you.
.TP
.B "-l, --chain=\fIrem1,rem2,rem3,..."
Use the defined chain to route the message through the Yamn network.  Random
nodes can be selected with asterisks. E.g. --chain="*,*,*".
A random node is never a remailer used within
.B stats.distance
hops of it, including the named ones.
If multiple copies are specified, all copies must share a common exit remailer.
.TP
.B "-m, --mail"
Operate in client mode and inject a plain-text message into the Yamn pool.
A message is sent in chunks of 17910 bytes, at most 255 of them (about
4.5 MB with its headers); a larger message is refused.
.TP
.B "-M, --remailer"
Operate in server mode.  Normally used in combination with 
.B "-D"
to continuously process, otherwise only a single iteration of read, process,
send  will be performed.
.TP
.B "--meminfo"
In server mode, log the process's memory use when it starts and with the
hourly statistics (needs loglevel info).
.TP
.B "--nodummy"
Don't send dummy messages.
.TP
.B "-R, --read-mail"
Read an incoming mail from STDIN and store it in the remailer's Maildir, as an
MTA would deliver it.  STDIN may also hold an mbox of several mails, such as
the output of
.BR --stdout ,
which is split into one Maildir message per mail.
.TP
.B "--refresh"
Download the public keyring and statistics now (from
.B urls.pubring
and
.BR urls.mlist2 ,
or from every pinger in
.BR urls.pingers )
and exit, also with
.BR "urls.fetch: false" .
A download only replaces the current file if it reads as a keyring
with at least one key valid now, or as statistics with at least one remailer.
An https URL isn't followed to a plain http one.
.IP
With
.B "urls.fetch: true"
(the default),
.B -m
downloads the files right before sending when they are more than an hour
old: the pingers see your IP address and when you send, and a pinger that
is down delays sending by up to 30 seconds per file.  To keep downloads
apart from sending, set
.B "urls.fetch: false"
and run
.B "yamn --refresh"
on a schedule at a random time, for example from cron with
.B "sleep $(shuf -i 0-3600 -n 1)"
before it, or from a systemd timer with
.BR RandomizedDelaySec .
.TP
.B "-s, --subject=\fIsubject"
Specify a Subject header for the message.  If this isn't defined, the Subject
is expected to be defined within the message.
.TP
.B "-S, --send"
Flush the outbound pool.  Useful for client mode and remailer testing but
should not be used on an in-production remailer.  Only one process sends from
a pool at a time (it locks
.IR pooldir /.lock);
if another one, such as a remailer started with
.BR --daemon ,
is sending, nothing is flushed.
.TP
.B "--stdout"
Write the encrypted mails to STDOUT instead of storing them in the Pool, so
they can be sent some other way.  The output is an mbox: one mail per copy and
chunk, each addressed to its first remailer.  No dummy message is sent.  A
remailer can read this output with
.BR -R .
.TP
.B "--import"
Read encrypted mails from STDIN, as written by
.BR --stdout ,
and store them in the Pool, to be sent with
.BR -S .
STDIN may hold an mbox or a single mail.  Each mail must carry a valid yamn
packet; others are reported and skipped.  This allows encrypting a message on
one machine and sending it from another, which never sees the plaintext:
.B "yamn -m --stdout < msg > out.mbox"
on the first,
.B "yamn --import -S < out.mbox"
on the second.
.TP
.B "-t, --to=\fIuser@host"
Specify a recipient for the message.  If this option isn't defined, the recipient
is expected to be included in the message itself.
.TP
.B "-V, --version"
Print the version and exit.  Versions of this fork are the upstream
(crooks/yamn) release they are based on plus
.RI +gw N ,
e.g. 0.2.7+gw1.  Encrypted mails carry only the upstream version, so
they don't tell which fork sent them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In client mode
.RB ( -m ),
yamn exits with 0 when the whole message was pooled (or written with
.BR --stdout ),
and with 1 when it was refused: for example an unknown remailer or a chain
that can't be built, no To, Cc or Bcc address, or more than two arguments.
Every chunk and copy is encrypted before the first one is pooled, so after
an error nothing of the message is sent.  A dummy message that can't be
built only gives a warning.
.SH CONFIGURATION
yamn reads a YAML configuration file.  It uses the first one it finds of:
.IP 1. 4
the file given with
.BR --config ;
.IP 2. 4
the file named by the
.B YAMNCFG
environment variable (if it's set, the file must exist);
.IP 3. 4
.I yamn.yml
in the
.B --dir
directory, if
.B --dir
is given;
.IP 4. 4
.IR ~/yamn.yml ;
.IP 5. 4
.IR /etc/yamn.yml .
.PP
A
.I yamn.yml
in the current directory is not read automatically (it could be planted by
someone else); use
.B "--config ./yamn.yml"
for that.  Without any config file, yamn uses its built-in defaults.
.PP
Options yamn doesn't know, such as a misspelt one, are reported as warnings
and ignored.  Values it can't use are errors, for example a negative
.BR stats.distance ,
.B stats.maxlat
below
.BR stats.minlat ,
an uptime outside 0 to 100 or
.B urls.key_quorum
below 1.
.PP
A config file only needs the options that differ from the defaults.  Options
are grouped in sections, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
remailer:
    name: myremailer
    address: myremailer@example.org
    exit: true
mail:
    smtp_relay: localhost
    mx_relay: false
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every option, with its default and a short description, is listed in
.IR examples/yamn.yml.defaults ;
.I examples/yamn.yml.client
and
.I examples/yamn.yml.remailer
are minimal starting points.
.B "yamn --debug"
prints the configuration in effect.  A path in the
.B files
section that starts with
.I ~/
is in your home directory.
.PP
yamn ignores a key in
.I pubring.mix
whose remailer name isn't made of lowercase letters and digits, or whose
address isn't a single local@domain of at most 52 characters.  Keys that share
a name but not the address, or the address but not the name, are ignored too.
.SS Stale statistics
Statistics older than
.B stats.stale_hours
(default 24) are still used to pick random remailers; yamn only warns about
them.  With several pingers, see below.
.SS Exit remailers
An exit delivers a message to at most
.B mail.max_recipients
addresses (default 10; 0 means no limit), counting To, Cc and Bcc after the
destination block list.  A message with more recipients is not delivered, so
one anonymous message can't be fanned out to hundreds of addresses.  The limit
is announced in the
.B remailer-conf
reply.
.SS Mail to .onion addresses
With
.B mail.tor_socks
set to a Tor SOCKS port, such as
.BR 127.0.0.1:9050 ,
yamn delivers mail for an address at a v3
.I .onion
domain through Tor to that onion service, on port 25.  This also works
where outgoing port 25 is blocked, and next to
.BR smtp_relay ,
which then gets the other recipients.  A
.I .onion
MX host is used too, and
.B smtp_relay
may be one.  yamn never looks up a
.I .onion
name in DNS, and gives each delivery its own Tor circuit.  TLS is used if
the onion service offers it, without checking the certificate, and is not
required by
.BR require_tls :
Tor encrypts the connection, and the address is the service's key.
Without
.BR tor_socks ,
mail to a
.I .onion
domain is refused.
.SS Several pingers
By default the keyring and statistics come from a single pinger
.RB ( urls.pubring
and
.BR urls.mlist2 ).
That pinger decides which key each remailer has.  With
.BR urls.pingers ,
yamn downloads the files of several pingers, kept as
.I pubring.mix.<name>
and
.IR mlist2.txt.<name> ,
and merges them into the usual
.I pubring.mix
and
.IR mlist2.txt :
.IP \(bu 2
A key is only used if at least
.B urls.key_quorum
pingers (default 2) publish it for the same remailer.  So one wrong, stale or
compromised pinger can neither add a key nor replace one.  If fewer pingers
have a usable keyring (for example on a first run, when some can't be
reached), nothing is merged and the current keyring is kept.  Set a lower
.B urls.key_quorum
to accept fewer;
.B "key_quorum: 1"
trusts any single pinger.
.IP \(bu 2
A key's version, capabilities and dates are those most of its pingers
publish.  On a tie, the more cautious value is used: middleman rather than
exit, the later valid-from and the earlier valid-until date.
.IP \(bu 2
A remailer name must belong to one address; if pingers disagree on that, the
address published by more pingers is used.
.IP \(bu 2
A remailer's statistics are the median of the pingers whose
.I mlist2.txt
is at most
.B stats.stale_hours
old.  They must come from as many pingers as a key; if fewer fresh ones list
the remailer, older ones fill in, newest first, and without enough of them the
remailer gets no statistics (so it isn't chosen at random).  Statistics dated
more than an hour in the future aren't used.  The merged file carries the
oldest Generated time used.
If every pinger's statistics are older than that, yamn warns.
.PP
Disagreements between pingers are logged (loglevel info), problems with a
pinger as warnings.  A pinger that can't be reached keeps its last downloaded
files.  Plain http URLs are warned about: anyone on the network path can
change what is downloaded from them.
.SS Where the files are
The relative paths of the
.B files
section (the default file names included) are relative to:
.IP 1. 4
the
.B --dir
directory, if given;
.IP 2. 4
else the directory of the config file, if it was given with
.B --config
or
.BR YAMNCFG .
If that directory has none of yamn's files (pubring.mix, secring.mix, the
pool) but the current directory has, the current directory is used, as in
earlier versions, with a warning;
.IP 3. 4
else the current directory, with a warning: a pubring.mix put there by
someone else would be used.
.PP
Earlier versions always used the current directory without
.BR --dir .
Setups that run yamn from its directory with
.B --dir
or with the config file in that directory are not affected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I ISSUES.md
in the source tree for known issues and their status.
